VALUES
  ('30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000034', '20000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000021', 'Paul''s Five Defense Speeches (Acts 21–26)', 1, 'Acts 21-26', '<h2>Scripture Reference</h2><blockquote><p>In Acts 21–26 we read of five defense speeches given by the apostle Paul and also learn what happens in between them. In the first two speeches Paul defends himself in Jerusalem before the Jewish crowd (22:1-21) and before the supreme Jewish council (23:1-6). His next three defenses are given in Caesarea. He goes before Felix in chapter 24, Festus in chapter 25, and finally King Herod Agrippa II in chapter 26. Because we, too, will face opposition, and because we must be good defenders of the faith like Paul, these chapters are relevant.</p><p>— <em>Christ-Centered Exposition Commentary</em> on Acts, pages 334–335</p></blockquote><h2>Overview</h2><p>Believers must be effective on <em>offense</em>, taking the gospel to the nations. But we also have to be effective on <em>defense</em>, "ready at any time to give a defense to anyone who asks . . . for a reason for the hope that is in [us]" (1 Pet 3:15).</p><p>For the last decade of his life Paul has intentionally engaged strategic centers with the gospel during his three missionary journeys. But now we''ll observe Paul playing defense. The apostle becomes a prisoner, and the rest of his ministry recorded in Acts is basically a set of reactions to opposition and assaults.</p><p>This session is a map of that whole stretch. Read it before or alongside the individual sessions on Acts 21, 22, and 23 so the group can see where each trial sits in the larger story — and so the repeated pattern becomes visible: <strong>Paul is handed one hostile audience after another, and he turns each one into an occasion to talk about the resurrection of Jesus.</strong></p><p><strong>Main idea:</strong> Across six chapters and five hearings, God moves Paul from a riot in Jerusalem to an appeal before Caesar — and in every setting Paul''s task isn''t mainly to defend himself but to testify about Jesus.</p><h2>Key Points</h2><h3>The Setting: From Missionary to Prisoner (21:17-40)</h3><p>Paul arrives in Jerusalem, is welcomed by James and the elders, and joins four men completing a Nazirite vow in order to demonstrate his respect for his Jewish heritage (21:17-26). Asian Jews recognize him in the temple, raise the false charge that he has defiled it, and a mob forms (21:27-36). Lysias the Roman commander arrests him and puts him in "two chains" (v. 33). At the top of the barracks stairs Paul asks permission to speak — and the crowd falls silent (21:37-40).</p><p>Everything that follows happens to a man in Roman custody. From here to the end of Acts, Paul is a prisoner.</p><h3>Speech 1: Before the Jewish Crowd in Jerusalem (22:1-21)</h3><p>Paul''s "defense" (<em>apologia</em>, 22:1) is a respectful and honest account of his life and conversion. He had two purposes: to show that he was <strong>loyal</strong> to his Jewish heritage, and to give the <strong>honest</strong> facts of his conversion — that his ministry wasn''t the result of his own imagination and wild ideas but of the sovereign, transforming grace of Jesus.</p><ul><li>He recounts his former zeal as a strict Pharisee who persecuted Christians to the death (22:3-5).</li><li>He recounts meeting the living Christ on the Damascus road (22:6-11) — Jesus <em>of Nazareth</em>, who told him that in persecuting the church he was persecuting the Lord himself.</li><li>He recounts his commission through Ananias, a man with "a good reputation with all the Jews" (22:12-16).</li><li>He recounts his vision in the temple (22:17-21) — an answer to the very charge that he had defiled it.</li></ul><p>The speech ends the moment Paul says the word "Gentiles" (22:21-22). The mob erupts, and Paul never gets to address the temple charge. <strong>Paul used this defense opportunity as an occasion to speak the good news.</strong></p><h3>Between the Speeches: "Paper" (22:22-29)</h3><p>Lysias prepares to have Paul flogged. Paul asks one question — "Is it legal for you to scourge a man who is a Roman citizen and is uncondemned?" (v. 25) — and the examination stops. Paul was a citizen by birth; Lysias had bought his citizenship.</p><h3>Speech 2: Before the Sanhedrin (23:1-10)</h3><p>Paul opens by appealing to his own upright character. The high priest Ananias orders him struck on the mouth; Paul answers with a prophetic rebuke, calling him a "whitewashed" wall, then steps back from the manner of his words when told he has spoken against a ruler of the people (23:2-5). He then names the real issue — "the hope of the resurrection" (v. 6) — and the council splits, because the Pharisees believed in resurrection and the Sadducees did not. <strong>The Pharisees actually defended Paul in this case!</strong> The shouting turns violent, and Lysias again rescues him.</p><p>That night the Lord Jesus stands by Paul and tells him he must testify in Rome as he has in Jerusalem (23:11) — a reminder that Paul''s task wasn''t mainly to defend himself; he was to testify about Jesus.</p><h3>Between the Speeches: The Plot and the Transfer (23:12-35)</h3><p>More than forty men swear not to eat or drink until they have killed Paul. Paul''s unnamed nephew hears of it and reports it; Lysias sends Paul to Caesarea under guard of two hundred infantrymen, seventy horsemen, and two hundred "spearmen," with a letter stating that the dispute is theological and warrants neither death nor imprisonment. <strong>Never mistake the lack of the spectacular for the inactivity of God. His quiet, invisible hand is always at work.</strong></p><h3>Speech 3: Before Felix the Governor (24:1-27)</h3><p>Chapter 24 gives a typical court procedure — the filing of charges, the prosecution of the plaintiff''s spokesman, and the answer from the defendant (Johnson, <em>Let''s Study Acts</em>, 293).</p><p>The prosecution hires a professional orator, Tertullus, who flatters Felix for a "great peace" his reign never produced, then brings four charges: Paul is a <em>pest</em> — a "plague" (v. 5); a <em>political agitator</em> who stirs up riots; <em>the leader of a sectarian movement</em> — the only place in the New Testament where the term "Nazarenes" describes Christians (Marshall, <em>Acts of the Apostles</em>, 375); and <em>disruptive in the temple</em> (v. 6). In a sentence, Tertullus charges, "Paul disturbs the peace as a seditious member of a dangerous sect" (Bock, <em>Acts</em>, 691).</p><p>Paul''s <strong>public witness</strong> has three parts (Gangel, <em>Acts</em>, 411):</p><ol><li><strong>A clear religious record (24:11-16).</strong> He has been in Jerusalem only twelve days — it''s difficult to start a riot in less than two weeks — and he takes pains to have a "clear conscience toward God and men" (v. 16).</li><li><strong>Blameless civil behavior (24:17-20).</strong> He came bringing "charitable gifts" and presented "offerings" at the temple. His accusers from Asia are conspicuously absent from the trial; they had no case.</li><li><strong>A personal message: Jesus is alive (24:21).</strong> Once again Paul funnels the hearing to the resurrection.</li></ol><p>Then comes a <strong>private context</strong> (24:24-26). Felix postpones a decision and later sends for Paul, who speaks the same message in private that he spoke in public: "faith in Christ Jesus" (v. 24). He reasons about righteousness, self-control, and the coming judgment (v. 25) — three topics fitting Felix''s lifestyle exactly. <strong>The sermon, then, was not a nice, warm, uplifting talk. It involved confrontation with a call to repentance.</strong> Felix was convicted by what he had heard but not changed. He kept summoning Paul, hoping for a bribe, and left him in prison two years.</p><h3>Speech 4: Before Festus, and the Appeal to Caesar (25:1-27)</h3><p>Felix is replaced by Festus. The Jewish leaders press their charges again and ask for Paul to be transferred to Jerusalem, secretly intending to kill him on the way (25:1-5). At the judgment seat the accusers bring many serious charges they cannot prove, and Paul denies offending against the law, the temple, or Caesar (25:6-8).</p><p>When Festus — wanting to do the Jewish leaders a favor — proposes a trial in Jerusalem, Paul refuses and appeals to Caesar (25:9-12). It is the decisive turn of the whole section: the appeal is what will carry him to Rome, exactly as the Lord had said in 23:11.</p><p>King Agrippa II and Bernice then arrive on a state visit. Festus lays the case before Agrippa, admitting the dispute concerns the Jewish religion and one Jesus whom Paul claims is alive, and Agrippa asks to hear Paul himself (25:13-22). The hearing is convened with great ceremony, and Festus concedes publicly that Paul has done nothing deserving death — while admitting he has no charge to send to the emperor (25:23-27).</p><h3>Speech 5: Before King Agrippa II (26:1-32)</h3><p>Paul''s final and fullest defense. Given leave to speak, he addresses a king he describes as expert in Jewish customs and questions (26:1-3), and then traces his life:</p><ul><li><strong>His upbringing</strong> — those who knew him from youth can testify he lived as a Pharisee, within the strictest sect (26:4-5).</li><li><strong>The real charge</strong> — he stands on trial over the hope of the promise God made to the fathers, the hope the twelve tribes pursue night and day (26:6-8).</li><li><strong>His persecution of Christians</strong> — he imprisoned believers by the chief priests'' authority, cast his vote against them, and pursued them even to foreign cities (26:9-11).</li><li><strong>The Damascus road</strong> — at noon a light brighter than the sun, and a voice in Hebrew asking why he persecuted, identifying himself as Jesus (26:12-15).</li><li><strong>His commission</strong> — appointed a servant and witness, sent to Jews and Gentiles to open their eyes, turning them from darkness to light and from the power of Satan to God, that they might receive forgiveness and an inheritance through faith (26:16-18).</li><li><strong>His obedience and message</strong> — not disobedient to the heavenly vision, he preached repentance and deeds to match, testifying nothing beyond what Moses and the prophets foretold: that the Christ would suffer, and that by his resurrection he would bring light to Israel and to the Gentiles (26:19-23).</li></ul><p>Festus interrupts, calling him out of his mind; Paul answers that he is speaking words of truth and appeals to Agrippa, since none of this was done in a corner (26:24-27). Agrippa deflects with a question about becoming a Christian so quickly, and Paul answers that he wishes all his hearers were as he is — except for the chains (26:28-29). Withdrawing, the officials agree that Paul has done nothing deserving death or imprisonment, and Agrippa observes that he could have been released had he not appealed to Caesar (26:30-32).</p><h2>Scripture Deep Dive</h2><p>Three threads run through all five hearings, and they are worth tracing with the group.</p><p><strong>First, the resurrection is always the point.</strong> Paul''s belief in Jesus''s <em>resurrection</em> separated him from the rest of the Jews, and throughout his defense speeches he constantly brings up the resurrection (cf. 24:14-16; 26:6-8; 28:20). It is the hinge of speech 2 (23:6), the conclusion of speech 3 (24:21), and the climax of speech 5 (26:23).</p><blockquote><p>If Christ has not been raised, then our proclamation is in vain, and so is your faith.</p><p>— 1 Corinthians 15:14</p></blockquote><p><strong>Second, Paul is repeatedly declared innocent.</strong> Lysias''s letter says the matter doesn''t warrant death or imprisonment (23:29); Festus says the same (25:25-27); Agrippa and his court say it again (26:32). Luke weaves this theme into Acts: Christians aren''t dangerous law-breakers (Schreiner, "Living as Disciples").</p><p><strong>Third, God is steering the whole thing.</strong> The Lord told Paul in the barracks that he must testify in Rome (23:11). A nephew overhears a plot; a commander mobilizes an army; a governor stalls for two years; a new governor offers a Jerusalem trial that forces an appeal to Caesar. <strong>Marvel at the sovereignty of God here.</strong></p><blockquote><p>Sometimes God delivers his children by the simple word of a young relative. Sometimes he has to call in the cavalry. At all times, he is ultimately in charge.</p><p>— Kenneth Gangel, <em>Acts</em>, 391</p></blockquote><h2>Discussion Questions</h2><ol><li>Trace Paul''s five audiences: a religious mob, a religious court, a corrupt governor, a new governor, and a king. What changes in how Paul speaks, and what stays the same?</li><li>Why do you think Paul brings up the resurrection in every single hearing?</li><li>In each of the five settings, Paul was the accused. How does he manage to keep turning the conversation to Jesus rather than to himself?</li><li>Three separate Roman authorities declare Paul innocent, and he stays in prison anyway. What does that teach us about the relationship between vindication and God''s purposes?</li><li>Compare Felix (convicted but unchanged, 24:25) with Agrippa (deflecting with a question, 26:28). What are the ways people today keep the gospel at arm''s length?</li><li>Paul told Agrippa he had not been "disobedient to the heavenly vision" (26:19). What has God made clear to you that obedience now requires?</li><li>Where in your own life are you being called to give an answer for the hope within you — and what would calm, courageous faithfulness look like there?</li><li>Take a few moments to pray for Christians who are on trial today for their witness.</li></ol><h2>Application</h2><p><strong>Be ready on defense, not just offense.</strong> Usually the focus of fulfilling our Christian mission is on offense — initiating gospel conversations with unbelievers. But sometimes unbelievers may actually start gospel conversations with us, and in those situations we must be ready to explain why we''re Christians.</p><p><strong>Look for the transition.</strong> Paul first dealt with the charges and then gave a defense of the faith. He doesn''t merely want to defend himself; he wants to proclaim the gospel as well. This is an excellent reminder to look for ways in your daily conversations to make gospel transitions as you talk with people.</p><p><strong>Expect opposition to be skillful.</strong> Those who oppose Christ will go to great lengths to oppose the kingdom. They will use skill, political maneuvering, and lies. Such opposition shouldn''t surprise us. We should be prepared spiritually for such attacks.</p><p><strong>Speak the whole message, including the hard parts.</strong> Paul reasoned with Felix about righteousness, self-control, and the coming judgment. None of these points were popular then, nor are they today. But we must be more concerned about offending Christ than culture. Speak his Word in humility and love as a faithful messenger.</p><p><strong>Trust the sovereign hand you cannot see.</strong> God has an infinite number of options for working out his will in our lives. While our daily lives may not look spectacular, we can be assured that God is involved in the affairs of his people. <strong>While we still have breath, we should believe that Jesus has work for us to do.</strong></p><hr><h2>Notes</h2><p><strong>Purpose.</strong> This session is a context-setting overview for the sessions on Acts 21, 22, and 23. It is meant to be taught first, or used as a handout, so the group can place each trial within the whole arc of Acts 21–26.</p><p><strong>Sources and provenance — please read before teaching.</strong> This overview draws on two different kinds of material, and they are not equally sourced:</p><ul><li><strong>Acts 21:17–24:27 (the setting, speeches 1–3, and the material in between)</strong> is adapted from <em>Christ-Centered Exposition Commentary</em> on Acts, pages 334–365 — the same pages behind the Acts 21, 22, and 23 sessions. Quotations, citations, and italicized terms in those portions are the commentary''s own.</li><li><strong>Acts 25–26 (speeches 4 and 5)</strong> is summarized <strong>from the narrative of Acts itself</strong>, because the commentary pages covering chapters 25–26 were not part of the supplied material. Those two sections deliberately contain <strong>no quotations and no secondary citations</strong> — only the movement of the biblical text with verse references, so nothing is attributed to the commentary that it did not say. Verse divisions were checked against the text of Acts 25–26. If the commentary pages for chapters 25–26 become available, those two sections should be expanded the way the others were.</li></ul><p><strong>The commentary''s own roadmap</strong> appears on pages 334–335 and is quoted verbatim in the Scripture Reference block above. Note that it identifies the Sanhedrin speech as 23:1-6; the Acts 23 session treats the fuller unit 23:1-10.</p><p><strong>Related sessions.</strong> Acts 21 (pages 334–340) · Acts 22 (pages 340–349) · Acts 23 (pages 349–360). Acts 24 material appears on pages 360–365 and is summarized here under Speech 3, but has not yet been built out as its own session.</p><p><strong>Works cited in this session:</strong> Johnson, <em>Let''s Study Acts</em> · Marshall, <em>Acts of the Apostles</em> · Bock, <em>Acts</em> · Gangel, <em>Acts</em> · Schreiner, "Living as Disciples".</p>', 1, true, now()),
  ('30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000035', '20000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000021', 'Paul in Jerusalem — Humility, Love, and Hostility', 2, 'Acts 21:17-40', '<h2>Scripture Reference</h2><blockquote><p>Read Acts 21:17-40 together before the study. Quoted phrases and key moments in this session: "God had done" the work among the Gentiles (v. 19) · the Jewish believers "glorified God" (v. 20) · Paul was accused of defiling the temple (v. 28) · Lysias put Paul in "two chains" (v. 33) · the crowd chanted, "Get rid of him!" (v. 36)</p><p>— Acts 21:17-40</p></blockquote><h2>Overview</h2><p>Believers must be effective on <em>offense</em>, taking the gospel to the nations. But we also have to be effective on <em>defense</em>, "ready at any time to give a defense to anyone who asks . . . for a reason for the hope that is in [us]" (1 Pet 3:15). Usually the focus of fulfilling our Christian mission is on offense—initiating gospel conversations with unbelievers. But sometimes unbelievers may actually start gospel conversations with us, and in those situations we must be ready to explain why we''re Christians.</p><p>For the last decade of his life Paul has intentionally engaged strategic centers with the gospel during his three missionary journeys. But now we''ll observe Paul playing defense. The apostle becomes a prisoner, and the rest of his ministry recorded in Acts is basically a set of reactions to opposition and assaults. In Acts 21–26 we read of five defense speeches given by the apostle Paul and also learn what happens in between them.</p><p><strong>Main idea:</strong> Paul gets attacked by a Jewish mob while participating in a vow, and then he gives a defense speech before the crowd.</p><p>In this session we consider Paul''s welcome in Jerusalem (21:17-26) and the hostility of the Jewish crowd (21:27-36). In these we see an example of humility and love that we should pursue, and an example of hostility and lies that we may face.</p><h2>Key Points</h2><h3>Point 1: A God-Centered Celebration (21:18-20a)</h3><p>When Paul arrives in Jerusalem, things get interesting on day two. A formal meeting takes place between Paul, James (Jesus''s half brother and primary spokesman of the Jerusalem church), and the Jerusalem elders.</p><ul><li>James and Paul together praise God for the victories achieved among the Gentiles. "God had done" the work among them (v. 19). Paul didn''t brag; he gave glory to God.</li><li>He also related "in detail" what God did on his journeys. It''s a good practice to recount the evidences of God''s grace and to share them with others. The psalmist says, "My soul, bless the LORD, and do not forget all his benefits" (103:2 ESV).</li><li>When the Jewish brothers heard these things, they too "glorified God" (v. 20). The report concerned what God did among <em>the Gentiles</em>—and the Jewish Christians respond with joy, not jealousy or suspicion.</li></ul><p>At times it''s difficult for some Christians to rejoice when God is working through someone else or through someone else''s church. But that''s an attitude from which the follower of Jesus must repent. <strong>We are never in competition with other like-minded Christians; we''re on the same team!</strong></p><h3>Point 2: A Grace-Motivated Gift</h3><p>At some point, in response to the grace of Jesus, certain Christians presented a love offering to the church in Jerusalem. This gift accomplished two purposes:</p><ul><li>It served as a means of helping the poor (cf. Acts 11:27-30; 20:35; 2 Cor 8:9; Gal 2:10; 6:10).</li><li>It also served as a symbol of unity. In sending it, the Gentile Christians were humbly and lovingly expressing their solidarity with the Jewish Christians.</li></ul><p>Unfortunately the gift in this case didn''t actually accomplish the unifying purpose Paul hoped it would (Rom 15:31). Paul''s reputation was suffering, so James gives Paul an interesting proposal in hopes of disarming the suspicions.</p><h3>Point 3: A Gospel-Driven Plan (21:20b-26)</h3><p>The great news was that thousands of Jews trusted in Christ (v. 20b). But what hurt Paul''s reputation was the word on the street that he was urging Jewish Christians living in Gentile communities to abandon their heritage.</p><p>James''s concern was carefully limited:</p><ul><li>It wasn''t about <em>salvation</em>—he and Paul agreed that salvation was by grace alone through faith alone in Christ alone, apart from works of the law (see Acts 15; cf. 1 Cor 7:19; Gal 6:15).</li><li>It wasn''t about what Paul taught the <em>Gentiles</em>—the Jerusalem Council had already settled that (see 21:25; cf. 15:20,29).</li><li>It wasn''t about the <em>moral law</em>—both men agreed that regenerate people should pursue holiness.</li><li>The concern had to do with <em>Jewish cultural practices</em> (Stott, <em>Message of Acts</em>, 341).</li></ul><p>The rumors were untrue. Paul never demanded that the Jews abandon circumcision. In fact, his hurry to reach Jerusalem by Pentecost revealed that he still valued Israel''s calendar (Johnson, <em>Let''s Study Acts</em>, 266). Though Paul proclaimed that Christ was the end of the law (Rom 10:4), he didn''t insist that Jewish believers give up Jewish customs (Rom 14–15; 1 Cor 8–10).</p><p>So James proposed that Paul demonstrate his respect for traditional Jewish Christianity by participating in a vow, going with four brothers who were soon to complete a Nazirite vow (cf. Num 6:1-21) and paying for the animals for their sacrifice. By fulfilling this request (v. 26), Paul showed that he didn''t object to Jewish converts following Jewish customs as long as the gospel wasn''t compromised and the customs weren''t required of Gentiles.</p><p>Was this a compromise of the gospel? Paul''s actions are in keeping with his missionary policy of becoming "all things to all people" for the sake of the gospel''s spread (1 Cor 9:22-23). He lived out his words, "To the Jews I became like a Jew, to win Jews" (1 Cor 9:20). Paul could have said, "Let the haters hate. I''m going to do what I want!" But he didn''t.</p><h3>Point 4: Hostility and Lies Displayed in the Jewish Mob (21:27-36)</h3><p>From its focus on humility and love, Acts 21 turns to hostility and lies. What a stark contrast between the spirit of Paul and the spirit of the Jewish crowd.</p><ul><li>Some Asian Jews—probably from Ephesus (see 21:29)—recognize Paul and respond violently (vv. 27-28). Their wild accusations are designed to generate mob violence, and they sound like what Stephen''s accusers said before stoning him (6:13-14).</li><li>The accusations are ironic given that Paul was actually undergoing a purification ritual in order to enter the temple (in Stott, <em>Message of Acts</em>, 343).</li><li>The Jews assumed Paul had brought Trophimus the Ephesian (v. 29; cf. 20:4) beyond the wall separating the court of the Gentiles from the inner sanctuary (cf. Eph 2:14). Of course, he had done no such thing.</li><li>Lysias, the commander, arrests Paul and puts him in "two chains" (v. 33), perhaps indicating hand and foot, as Agabus predicted (v. 11). At the steps of the Antonia fortress the soldiers had to lift Paul up and carry him to protect him (v. 35).</li><li>The crowd chanted, "Get rid of him!" (v. 36), just as an earlier crowd in Jerusalem demanded of Pontius Pilate, "Take this man away!" (Luke 23:18) and "Crucify him!" (Mark 15:13).</li></ul><p>Did James''s plan backfire? Paul didn''t expect a pain-free experience in Jerusalem, for he knew afflictions awaited him there (cf. Acts 20:23; 21:4,10-11). His submission to James''s plan was designed to show his support to Jewish Christians committed to their Jewish heritage, not to escape hardship. Paul''s actions would eventually put the suspicions of many Jewish Christians to rest, and this event would be one link in the chain of sovereign events that would deliver Paul to Rome (Johnson, <em>Let''s Study Acts</em>, 268).</p><h3>Point 5: Permission to Speak (21:37-40)</h3><p>When Paul and the Roman soldiers reach the top of the stairs, Paul asks the tribune for permission to speak. The soldiers express surprise at Paul''s proficient Greek. Lysias assumes Paul might be the Egyptian assassin who had recently stirred up a revolt (v. 38)—a false prophet who, according to Josephus, presented himself as a new Moses or Joshua and predicted Jerusalem''s walls would fall like Jericho (in Polhill, <em>Acts</em>, 455).</p><p>Paul informs Lysias that he is neither a terrorist nor an Egyptian but a Jew and a citizen of the honorable city of Tarsus (v. 39), one of the great university cities in the Roman world (Stott, <em>Message of Acts</em>, 347). Upon learning of Paul''s distinguished background, Lysias grants him permission to speak. Paul stands at the top of the steps, and the crowd falls silent as he addresses them in their own language (21:40; cf. 22:2; 26:14).</p><h2>Scripture Deep Dive</h2><p>Why did Paul undergo a seven-day purification (v. 26)? John Polhill explains it was probably because Paul was returning from Gentile territory:</p><blockquote><p>Often a Jew on returning to the Holy Land after a sojourn in Gentile territory would undergo ritual purification. The period involved was seven days (cf. Num 19:12), which fits the present picture (v. 27). Paul thus underwent ritual purification to qualify for participation in the completion ceremony of the four Nazirites which took place within the sacred precincts of the temple. This would be a thorough demonstration of his full loyalty to the Torah, not only in his bearing the heavy expenses of the vow but also in his undergoing the necessary purification himself.</p><p>— Polhill, <em>Acts</em>, 449</p></blockquote><p>Since the minimum period for a Nazirite vow was thirty days, it seems Paul didn''t undergo the Nazirite vow himself. He had, however, previously taken such a vow in 18:18 as a traditional Jewish expression of thanksgiving or petition—which in the words of I. Howard Marshall would be "theologically acceptable" and not a violation of the gospel (<em>Acts of the Apostles</em>, 300).</p><h2>Discussion Questions</h2><ol><li>What does it mean to defend the faith?</li><li>When might you need to defend the faith?</li><li>How do Paul and James display humility and love?</li><li>Compare Acts 21:20b-26 with 1 Corinthians 9:15-23. Are Paul''s actions and his missionary policy consistent? Explain your answer.</li><li>Why were the Jewish people enraged at Paul?</li><li>In what modern contexts might Christians face hostility and false accusations? How should we respond to them?</li></ol><h2>Application</h2><p>We too should be flexible when ministering to various cultures. Some cultures are more traditional, while others are more progressive. When outside your immediate sphere, you may find it necessary to learn to adapt to the ways of another group for the sake of the gospel''s spread. <em>Never compromise the gospel</em> and <em>never participate in sin</em> when you are attempting to reach people, but don''t convey the impression that everyone must first be like you before they can take your invitation to accept Christ seriously. When the gospel is our main thing—when we find our identity in Christ rather than in an ethnic group, social class, or particular culture—then we will be able to minister humbly and lovingly with Pauline flexibility.</p><p>We must always make the right decisions in the sight of God and let things unfold as they may. Obedience to Jesus will involve hardship (2 Tim 3:12), so we shouldn''t be surprised should intimidation, hate, and false accusations come our way. Early Christians were accused of incest, cannibalism, and atheism simply because they greeted one another with a holy kiss, took the Lord''s Supper, and refused to worship the emperor. Today we''re accused of immorality and bigotry because of our views on marriage and life.</p><p>When falsely accused and persecuted, remember that the Suffering Servant is with you. Jesus stands ready to grant you grace in time of need, and he will have the last word.</p><p>Also, look for ways to bless others through tangible gifts. Christians should regularly display acts of generosity because we have received such amazing grace from Jesus.</p><hr><h2>Notes</h2><p><strong>Source.</strong> Adapted from <em>Christ-Centered Exposition Commentary</em> on Acts, the section "Paul in Jerusalem, Part 1" (Acts 21:17–22:21), pages 334–340. Paul''s defense speech itself (22:1-21), which begins on page 340, is covered in the Acts 22 session.</p><p><strong>Outline as given in the commentary:</strong></p><p>I. Humility and Love Displayed in James and Paul (21:17-26)<br>A. A God-centered celebration (21:18-20a)<br>B. A Grace-motivated gift<br>C. A Gospel-driven plan (21:20b-26)<br>II. Hostility and Lies Displayed in the Jewish Mob (21:27-36)<br>III. Honesty and Loyalty Displayed in Paul''s Defense (21:37–22:21)</p><p><strong>Works cited in this session:</strong> Stott, <em>Message of Acts</em> · Johnson, <em>Let''s Study Acts</em> · Marshall, <em>Acts of the Apostles</em> · Polhill, <em>Acts</em>.</p><p><strong>Cross-references gathered:</strong> 1 Pet 3:15; Ps 103:2; Acts 11:18,22-23,27-30; 14:27; 15:12,20,29; 20:23,35; 21:4,10-11,25; 2 Cor 8:9; Gal 2:10; 6:10,15; Rom 15:31; 1 Cor 7:19; 9:19-23; Acts 15; Rom 10:4; Rom 14–15; 1 Cor 8–10; Rom 2:25-30; Gal 4:9; 5:6; Rom 9:1-5; Num 6:1-21; 19:12; Acts 16:3; 18:18; 20:6; 23:5; 20:24; Phil 2:3-11; Acts 6:13-14; Eph 2:14; Luke 23:18; Mark 15:13; 2 Tim 3:12.</p>', 2, true, now()),
  ('30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000036', '20000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000024', 'Paul''s Defense — Honesty, Loyalty, and a Roman Citizen', 1, 'Acts 22', '<h2>Scripture Reference</h2><blockquote><p>Read Acts 22 together before the study. Quoted phrases and key moments in this session: Paul''s "defense" (<em>apologia</em>, v. 1) · "Brothers and fathers" (v. 1) · the "God of our ancestors" called Paul (v. 14) · God appointed Paul to see "the Righteous One" (v. 14) · Paul was called to be his "witness" to "all people" (v. 15) · "Get up and be baptized, and wash away your sins, calling on his name" (v. 16) · "Go, because I will send you far away to the Gentiles" (v. 21) · "Is it legal for you to scourge a man who is a Roman citizen and is uncondemned?" (v. 25)</p><p>— Acts 22</p></blockquote><h2>Overview</h2><p>In Paul''s "defense" (<em>apologia</em>, 22:1), he provides a respectful and honest account of his life and conversion in response to the charges made against him. He has almost been killed. He has been arrested and chained. But he speaks calmly.</p><p>Paul had two purposes in addressing the crowd. First, he wanted them to hear that he was loyal to his Jewish heritage. He saw himself as standing in continuity with it though he confessed Jesus as Messiah, the Righteous One foretold by the prophets. Second, Paul wanted them to know the facts about his conversion. His life and ministry weren''t the result of his own imagination and wild ideas. They were the result of the sovereign, transforming grace of Jesus. <strong>Paul used this defense opportunity as an occasion to speak the good news.</strong></p><p>His loyalty is conveyed in the careful way he addresses the crowd ("Brothers and fathers"), in his choice of language (22:2), in his mention of being raised in Jerusalem and educated under Gamaliel, in his reference to Ananias''s ministry (vv. 12-16), in his reference to the "God of our fathers," and in his statement of his vision in the temple (vv. 17-20). The apostle''s honesty is simply displayed in the telling of his testimony.</p><h2>Key Points</h2><h3>Point 1: Former Zeal (22:3-5)</h3><p>Paul begins by identifying with the crowd, reflecting on his former life.</p><ul><li>He was born a Jew in Tarsus and had been brought up in Jerusalem. He sat at the feet of Gamaliel.</li><li>He had been a strict Pharisee, indeed a Pharisee of Pharisees (Phil 3:4-7).</li><li>His zeal was expressed in the violent persecution of Christians, both men and women, whom he had handed over to death (cf. 8:3; 26:10). <strong>Paul wanted to destroy Christianity.</strong></li><li>The high priest and council of elders would confirm his story (22:5). They knew that at one time Paul had even sought to travel all the way to Damascus to persecute followers of the Way.</li></ul><h3>Point 2: Encounter with Jesus (22:6-11)</h3><p>But then something happened: Paul met the living Christ, and that changed everything.</p><p>The light blinding Paul around noon implies it outshone the sun at its strongest time (cf. 26:13, perhaps echoing Deut 28:28-29). Paul fell to the ground and heard Jesus speaking to him. Paul includes in his defense speech that it was Jesus of <em>Nazareth</em> who confronted him—before this Jewish audience, Paul wanted to be clear about Jesus''s identity. Jesus informed Paul that he had not only been persecuting the church but also the Lord himself in his eagerness to stamp out Christianity (cf. 9:4-5; 26:14-15). <strong>This truth highlights the special relationship between Christ and the church.</strong></p><p>Jesus didn''t execute Paul on the Damascus road. Instead, he turned the terrorist into an evangelist by his amazing grace (cf. Gal 1:13-17; 1 Tim 1:12-17). Paul was commissioned, not consumed.</p><h3>Point 3: Commission (22:12-16)</h3><p>Paul describes Ananias''s devotion to the law, adding that Ananias "had a good reputation with all the Jews" (v. 12). Paul''s omission of some of the story recorded in Acts 9 reveals part of his purpose: he wants to emphasize his respect for Israel. The person who led Paul into the Christian faith was himself well known for keeping God''s commands.</p><p>There are four parts to Paul''s commission:</p><ol><li><strong>The "God of our ancestors" called Paul (22:14).</strong> This is the language God used in Moses''s burning bush experience (Exod 3:15-16). Did Moses deserve to hear God''s voice? No. Did Paul deserve such grace? No. Both men were dependent on grace.</li><li><strong>God appointed Paul to see "the Righteous One" (22:14).</strong> This too is an allusion to the Old Testament, stressing the continuity between the Old Testament and the work of Jesus the Messiah. Isaiah speaks of this Righteous One as the obedient Servant who was wounded for our transgressions (Isa 53:5-6,11; cf. Acts 3:14; 7:52). The only way for a person to be righteous is through the Righteous One—not through religious effort.</li><li><strong>God called Paul to be his "witness" to "all people" (22:15; cf. v. 21; 26:17; Gal 1:16).</strong> The man would give eyewitness testimony (1 Cor 9:1) to people of all ethnicities and social classes. He told the Corinthians that he was compelled to preach this message (1 Cor 9:16).</li><li><strong>Paul received instructions on how to act on his commission.</strong> Ananias tells Paul to "get up and be baptized, and wash away your sins, calling on his name" (22:16). Baptism is the expression of one''s faith in Jesus. Calling on the name of Jesus is a cry for salvation, rescue, cleansing, and new life (cf. Rom 10:13).</li></ol><h3>Point 4: Vision in the Temple (22:17-21)</h3><p>In the last part of his defense speech, Paul tells of the vision he had in the temple. This part of Paul''s story doesn''t appear in Acts 9 (cf. 9:26-30). He probably included it here in order to answer the mob''s charge that he had defiled the temple. <strong>He had no interest in defiling the temple; he prayed in it.</strong></p><ul><li>In some ways Paul''s vision sounds similar to Isaiah''s vision (Isa 6:1-13), once again showing continuity between Judaism and the Christian faith. Both men were called through a vision and commissioned. Both were told that people would reject their messages. Isaiah was told to stay in the city to face the rejection, but Paul was told to leave (22:18; Polhill, <em>Acts</em>, 462).</li><li>Paul describes how he protested the vision, another element common to calling narratives in the Old Testament. He argued he should stay in Jerusalem because he was now the Lord''s convincing witness (vv. 19-20).</li><li>Despite the argument the Lord persisted: "Go, because I will send you far away to the Gentiles" (v. 21). This calling wasn''t merely for the sake of safety but as a matter of purpose (cf. 9:15-16).</li></ul><p>With the mention of the Gentiles, the mob''s suppressed emotion erupted (22:22). Paul didn''t get to deal with the charges of defiling the temple.</p><h3>Point 5: "Paper" — Paul Reveals His Roman Citizenship (22:22-29)</h3><p>After the crowd rages at Paul, expressing their desire to rip him to shreds, Lysias takes him away for examination (vv. 22-24), planning to have the apostle flogged. But just before the beating begins, Paul asks a centurion an attention-grabbing question: "Is it legal for you to scourge a man who is a Roman citizen and is uncondemned?" (v. 25). Paul knew, of course, that it was illegal to flog a Roman citizen prior to allowing him a formal trial or sentencing (cf. 16:37). His claim of citizenship could have been verified in the public records of Tarsus (Johnson, <em>Let''s Study Acts</em>, 278).</p><ul><li>The attending centurion immediately halts the plans and quickly reports the problem to the commander (22:26).</li><li>Lysias finds out that Paul is actually a Roman citizen—not a Johnny-come-lately to citizenship, like himself, who became a citizen <em>by bribe</em>, but a man who became one <em>by birth</em>.</li><li>How was Paul a Roman citizen by birth? There are several theories, including the idea that the Romans may have awarded Paul''s family citizenship because their tent-making trade proved useful to the military.</li><li>The examination ceases, and Lysias still doesn''t know the truth about Paul. Added to his frustration is a sense of fear (v. 29). He will put a positive spin on his actions later (see 23:27).</li></ul><p><strong>Paul urged Christians to submit to the governing authorities as God''s ministers of justice (Rom 13:1-7), but he also expected the government to exercise its duties rightly.</strong> This suggests there''s a difference between humbly suffering for Christ and being a victim of injustice. We live in a land of laws, and if the laws protect us, then we should appeal to them. If these laws prevent us from following Jesus, then it''s better to obey God than man (Acts 5:29).</p><p>Lysias then tries a third approach: he decides to let the religious council examine Paul (22:30)—apparently as he listens in.</p><h2>Scripture Deep Dive</h2><p>Lysias intended to examine Paul by scourging. John Polhill notes the violent nature of this form of torture:</p><blockquote><p>This was a particularly cruel manner of scourging that consisted of a beating across the raw flesh with leather thongs in which were inserted rough pieces of bone or metal. The thongs were set in a stout wooden handle. This was a much more severe manner of beating than that of the rods which Paul and Silas underwent at Philippi (16:22f., 37; cf. 2 Cor 11:25). It was not uncommon for the victim to die as a result of the flagellum.</p><p>— Polhill, <em>Acts</em>, 464</p></blockquote><p>This is why Paul''s single question in verse 25 changes everything. And it is worth remembering what Luke does <em>not</em> do here: Paul''s actions are simply reported, and Luke offers no appraisal of them. What we can say for certain is that Paul is clearly intelligent, savvy, bold, and passionate in front of the commander and the religious council. One thing is for sure: <strong>Paul is a man with feet of clay.</strong> In this chapter we see his humanity and his need for the Lord''s grace. It''s a need we all share.</p><h2>Discussion Questions</h2><ol><li>Generally speaking, what does Paul try to accomplish in his defense speech in Acts 22:1-21?</li><li>How is Paul''s calling similar to the callings of Moses and Isaiah? How is it different?</li><li>Do you think Paul''s speech was effective? Explain.</li><li>Do you think Paul should have allowed himself to be flogged instead of playing his Roman citizenship card? Explain your reasoning.</li><li>What about Paul''s defense most resonates with you?</li><li>Take a few moments to pray for God to help you present the gospel this week. Ask God to bring your hearers to faith in Christ.</li></ol><h2>Application</h2><p>Three features stand out from Paul''s defense:</p><p><em>First, we can learn from Paul''s calmness.</em> In this intense moment Paul responds with meekness, gentleness, and compassion. He doesn''t respond in anger. He doesn''t attack. He reasons. When you are in a pressure-filled conversation, ask the Father to calm you and give you grace to speak the message with gentleness and respect (cf. Gal 5:22-23; 2 Tim 2:24-25; 1 Pet 3:15).</p><p><em>Second, we can learn from Paul''s courage.</em> In the face of opposition, Paul didn''t bend. He stood his ground. Such boldness also comes from God (cf. 4:29-31). Many Christians can remain calm, but that''s because they cave in and fail to speak truth when pressed. We need both truth and love (cf. Eph 4:15). Only churches that love people <em>and</em> stand firmly on the gospel against the winds of culture will have anything to offer this broken world.</p><p><em>Third, we can learn from Paul''s calling.</em> Paul knew his assignment: proclaim the good news. That''s our job too. On this particular occasion, the crowd is enraged against the apostle. On other occasions in Acts, however, people respond to his words in faith. <strong>We aren''t responsible for the results of what happens when we lovingly proclaim the good news; we are responsible for sharing it.</strong></p><p>Some plant the seeds of the gospel through proclamation and others water it through loving actions and deeper teaching, but only God gives growth (1 Cor 3:6). Sometimes people will be enraged by a presentation of the gospel; sometimes people will hear it and inquire for more information; and sometimes people will surrender to the lordship of Jesus—though they may take their time doing so. We must remember that no one is beyond the reach of God''s converting grace. Just look at Paul! Just look at us! Be faithful to sow the seed and beg God to send rain to nourish it as you humbly and courageously give an answer for the hope within you.</p><hr><h2>Notes</h2><p><strong>Source.</strong> Adapted from <em>Christ-Centered Exposition Commentary</em> on Acts: the close of "Paul in Jerusalem, Part 1" (Acts 21:17–22:21), pages 340–345, and the opening of "Paul in Jerusalem, Part 2" (Acts 22:22–23:11), pages 346–349.</p><p><strong>Commentary outline for the defense speech:</strong></p><p>III. Honesty and Loyalty Displayed in Paul''s Defense (21:37–22:21)<br>A. Former zeal (22:3-5)<br>B. Encounter with Jesus (22:6-11)<br>C. Commission (22:12-16)<br>D. Vision in the temple (22:17-21)</p><p><strong>Illustration available for the "Paul''s calling" application (pages 344–345).</strong> The commentary tells the story of a student ("Kyle") who was an outspoken atheist in a Christian academy, whose friends and teachers "loved him well" and who heard the gospel repeatedly, and who eventually placed his faith in Jesus at the island of Patmos during a senior class trip through the Mediterranean. It pairs this with the story of a dying billionaire at a North Carolina country club who asked, "what good is all that now?" but who did not profess faith—the two men parted amicably and stayed in touch by letter. "At least I know he has heard the gospel," the teacher says. "I can''t make him respond." Include or drop as time allows.</p><p><strong>Textual note.</strong> On page 343 the commentary''s citation list for gentleness reads "Jas 4:17-18." This was verified against a high-resolution scan of the page and is printed that way in the book; James 3:17-18 appears to be intended. It is therefore omitted from the application above rather than silently corrected.</p><p><strong>Works cited in this session:</strong> Polhill, <em>Acts</em> · Johnson, <em>Let''s Study Acts</em> · Marshall, <em>Acts of the Apostles</em> · Stott, <em>Message of Acts</em>.</p>', 1, true, now()),
  ('30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000037', '20000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000023', 'Jesus Stands with Us — Before the Council and Under God''s Sovereign Hand', 1, 'Acts 23', '<h2>Scripture Reference</h2><blockquote><p>Read Acts 23 together before the study. Quoted phrases and key moments in this session: Ananias commanded those near Paul to "strike him on the mouth" (v. 2) · Paul called him a "whitewashed" wall (v. 3) · "You must not speak evil of a ruler of your people" (v. 5; citing Exod 22:28) · "the hope of the resurrection" (v. 6) · "Have courage! . . . You have testified about me in Jerusalem" (v. 11) · the conspirators'' oath not to eat or drink until they had murdered Paul (vv. 12-13) · Lysias took the young man "by the hand" (v. 19) · two hundred infantrymen, seventy mounted soldiers, and two hundred "spearmen" (v. 23)</p><p>— Acts 23</p></blockquote><h2>Overview</h2><p><strong>Main Idea:</strong> Jesus was with Paul when he testified before the authorities, and Jesus is with us when we speak the gospel before unbelieving and unfriendly audiences.</p><p>It''s one thing to know what to say about Jesus and how to say it, but there''s also the challenge of finding the strength and encouragement to proclaim it. In this passage we see that the same Jesus who stood for Paul at the cross now stands by Paul in his trials (23:11). Jesus stood for us, and he now stands with us.</p><p>In this portion of Paul''s afflictions in Jerusalem, the message of the book of 2 Corinthians comes to mind: "strength through weakness." We indeed see Paul afflicted, perplexed, persecuted, and struck down; but he''s not crushed, driven to despair, abandoned, or destroyed. He knows Jesus is with him. <strong>This section of Acts points us to the all-sufficient grace of Jesus, who uses wounded soldiers to accomplish his mission.</strong></p><p>It''s not an easy task to understand or teach this passage because Paul''s actions are simply reported here. Luke offers no appraisal of them. Bible teachers go in various directions when it comes to 23:1-10. One thing is for sure: Paul is a man with feet of clay. In this chapter we see his humanity and his need for the Lord''s grace. It''s a need we all share.</p><p>The second half of the chapter shifts the camera to God. The names of God, Jesus, and the Holy Spirit aren''t mentioned in 23:12-35—a notable absence that recalls the book of Esther, where the name of God is missing but his fingerprints are everywhere within the story. <strong>Never mistake the lack of the spectacular for the inactivity of God. His quiet, invisible hand is always at work.</strong></p><h2>Key Points</h2><h3>Point 1: A Clear Conscience before the Council (23:1)</h3><p>Paul "looked straight at" the Sanhedrin and offered his opening statement. First, he notes his own character—his actions are upright. The Sanhedrin included the high priestly aristocracy and the ruling elders, who were mainly Sadducees; the minority of members were Pharisees, represented among the scribes (Polhill, <em>Acts</em>, 470).</p><p>When it comes to defending the faith, our Christian character really matters (cf. 1 Tim 1:5; 1 Pet 3:14-16). We come to Jesus for a clean conscience (Heb 10:22). Paul told the Corinthians, "I am not conscious of anything against myself, but I am not justified by this. It is the Lord who judges me" (1 Cor 4:4). In what he said, Paul didn''t mean that he was sinless but that he lived above reproach and had done nothing to provoke the riot (see Acts 25:8). <strong>We should pursue a blameless life, and when we''re made aware of sin, we must humbly repent in order to maintain a good conscience and glorify God (see 24:16).</strong></p><h3>Point 2: "Rock" — Paul Rebukes the High Priest (23:2-5)</h3><p>No sooner did Paul testify to the uprightness of his own character than the high priest, Ananias, commanded those near Paul to "strike him on the mouth" (23:2; cf. John 18:22). What a bizarre interruption! One would expect a leader in the Jewish high court to display civility and justice. Ananias''s response was totally unacceptable and unbiblical (see Lev 19:15), and it makes his character stand out in stark contrast to Paul''s.</p><p>Why such anger and aggression?</p><ul><li>First, Ananias thought Paul a wicked man—a troublemaker. But Paul wouldn''t admit to any guilt, and this claim sounded absurd to Ananias, so he lost his temper.</li><li>Second, Ananias <em>was</em> a wicked man. According to historical records, he was known for greed, a quick temper, violence, and pro-Roman sentiments.</li></ul><p>Paul knew the law, and he knew that Ananias''s order was unjust (Lev 19:15). He therefore threw a verbal rock back at him, calling out the hypocrisy of the high priest (cf. Deut 28:22; Ezek 13:10-16; Matt 23:27), who was likely dressed in his fine religious attire. Paul''s language of a "whitewashed" wall was an insult: the priest outwardly looked clean and stable but was actually corrupt and flimsy. Paul''s statement was more prophetic than he probably realized; within ten years Jewish freedom fighters would assassinate Ananias (Polhill, <em>Acts</em>, 468).</p><p><strong>How should we read Paul''s words in verse 5?</strong> Interpreters take two main views:</p><ul><li>The <em>sincerity</em> view — Paul genuinely didn''t know he was speaking to the high priest. Maybe (1) Ananias wasn''t dressed in religious garb since this wasn''t a regular meeting of the Sanhedrin; (2) Paul didn''t know who gave the order because of the commotion; (3) Paul''s eyesight caused him to see only a blurry white-robed figure; or (4) Paul had been away from Jerusalem so long he didn''t know Ananias.</li><li>The <em>sarcasm</em> view — Paul was using irony, as if to say, "Brothers, I see nothing priestly in this man" (Lev 19:15; Deut 25:1; Calvin, "Christ the End of the Law," 318). If so, Paul is ministering in a prophetic sense: first the rebuke, then the strong statement about Ananias''s character.</li></ul><p>The commentary holds "(lightly!)" to the idea that Paul sincerely didn''t know that the high priest gave the order. He sought to address people respectfully, and when he crossed the line, he admitted it. We too should pursue the proper tone with ruling authorities (Bock, <em>Acts</em>, 674–75), refusing to mimic the disrespect so rampant in our culture. And should we fail to speak as we ought, we should emulate Paul''s humility.</p><p>While Paul spoke the truth, and while his cause was just, many correctly point out that his initial heated reaction doesn''t reflect the spirit of passages like 1 Corinthians 4:12: "When we are reviled, we bless; when we are persecuted, we endure it" (cf. Matt 5:39). Jesus and Paul both condemned hypocrisy, but at the time of his trial our Lord responded to his accusers with restraint (see Luke 22:63-71; John 18:19-23). <strong>While we must speak out against injustice, we must always do so with respect and restraint.</strong></p><h3>Point 3: "Scissors" — Paul Divides the Sadducees and Pharisees (23:6-10)</h3><p>The trial takes a sharp turn when Paul, still in the middle of his speech, brings up the reason for his arrest: "the hope of the resurrection" (v. 6). His words end up "dividing and conquering."</p><p>Was Paul trying to divert attention away from himself? Perhaps. But Paul did indeed point out the real issue behind his trials: it was a theological dispute. His belief in Jesus''s <em>resurrection</em> separated him from the rest of the Jews, and throughout his defense speeches he constantly brings up the resurrection (cf. 24:14-16; 26:6-8; 28:20). Further, at the end of this trial Jesus commended Paul for testifying about him in Jerusalem (23:11). So Paul was simply stating the facts.</p><ul><li><strong>Was Paul correct in calling himself a Pharisee and a son of Pharisees?</strong> He was in fact educated in the pharisaic tradition (cf. 22:3; Phil 3:5). He wasn''t necessarily claiming to endorse everything the Pharisees taught, but with respect to their eschatological hope revealed in Scripture (cf. Ezek 37:1-14; Dan 12:1-3), he still identified with them. <strong>Jesus was the fulfillment of that hope.</strong></li><li>The Sadducees didn''t believe in the resurrection, nor in <em>angels</em>, nor the <em>spirit</em> (23:8). They only recognized the books of Moses as authoritative and argued that such ideas weren''t found there. Jesus, however, showed them on one occasion that denying the resurrection contradicted God''s covenant faithfulness to the patriarchs (Luke 20:27-39; Johnson, <em>Let''s Study Acts</em>, 283).</li><li>The effect of the controversy was sharp division. <strong>The Pharisees actually defended Paul in this case!</strong> As the story unfolds, others will reach the same conclusion about Paul''s innocence (e.g., 23:29; 25:25-27; 26:32). But here the shouting soon turned to violence, and Lysias once again had to intervene and rescue Paul, delivering him back to the barracks.</li></ul><p>The resurrection remains central to the Christian faith. Paul said, "If Christ has not been raised, then our proclamation is in vain, and so is your faith" (1 Cor 15:14). We must keep heralding the good news of the empty tomb, and we should expect various responses to it.</p><h3>Point 4: Reassurance — Four Encouragements (23:11)</h3><p>Once Paul is back in the barracks, left to nurse his wounds and ponder what just happened, the Lord Jesus appears to the discouraged apostle. Paul was surely at a low point in his life: he had found an unsupportive church in Jerusalem, had suffered physically and emotionally before rulers and crowds, and may have questioned or regretted some of his actions. He was in need of the Lord''s grace. <strong>That''s just what he received.</strong> Jesus told Paul that he must go on to testify about him in Rome as he did in Jerusalem—a reminder that Paul''s task wasn''t mainly to defend himself; he was to testify about Jesus.</p><p><strong>1. The Lord knows us.</strong> Jesus knew Paul''s situation and condition, and he knows what we face, too. We are never outside his gaze. Jesus knows his sheep by name and by need (John 10:14).</p><p><strong>2. The Lord is with us.</strong> Jesus''s presence comforted Paul (cf. 18:9-10). While Paul felt alone, the reality is that Jesus was with him (cf. 2 Tim 4:17-18). Jesus stood for Paul at the cross; here he stands with Paul in the barracks. Be encouraged: the Lord will never leave you or forsake you (Heb 13:5).</p><p><strong>3. The Lord is for us.</strong> The Lord displays his support in two ways. First, an exhortation: "Have courage!" (cf. Matt 9:2,22; 14:27; John 16:33)—the same exhortation he gave Paul in Corinth (Acts 18:9). Second, a commendation: "You have testified about me in Jerusalem." What an encouragement to hear the Lord say, "You did it. You told people about Me!" Charles Spurgeon said that Paul was "too humble to console himself with that fact till his Lord gave him leave to do so by acknowledging the brave deed!" ("Paul Cheered in Prison"). Paul asked the Romans, "If God is for us, who is against us?" (Rom 8:31).</p><p><strong>4. The Lord isn''t finished with us.</strong> Paul may have wondered if he would make it to Rome. But in telling Paul it was "necessary" that he testify there, the sovereign Lord let him know he would make sure he arrived. Though Paul would encounter many trials en route, he could face them knowing that the Lord''s purposes would prevail. The psalmist said, "The course of my life is in your power" (Ps 31:15). <strong>While we still have breath, we should believe that Jesus has work for us to do.</strong></p><h3>Point 5: God Uses an Unnamed Nephew to Thwart a Plot (23:12-22)</h3><p>The day after Jesus reassured Paul in the barracks, more than forty angry Jews hatched a plot to kill him (vv. 12-13; cf. 9:24; 20:19). Their oath not to eat or drink until they had murdered the apostle meant they intended to act quickly. They approached the chief priests and elders—avoiding the minority Pharisees, who had defended Paul—and the Sanhedrin agreed to act as if they were reconvening to discuss Paul''s crimes, so that the assassins could intercept and execute him on the way. So much for justice and law within the Sanhedrin (Gangel, <em>Acts</em>, 390)! These terrorists, like many modern-day terrorists, would stop at nothing to achieve their selfish, religious goals.</p><p>God, however, would thwart the scheme—and he used a young man to do it. John Polhill says it''s anyone''s guess how old the nephew was, but perhaps he was in his late teens (<em>Acts</em>, 472); in light of how Lysias takes him "by the hand" in verse 19, he may be just a little boy (Schreiner, "Living as Disciples"). Regardless, this unnamed nephew becomes an incredibly important advocate, one clearly raised up "for such a time as this" (Esth 4:14).</p><p>This is also the only mention of Paul''s family found anywhere in the New Testament. We get the impression from his writings that he lost connection with them after his conversion (cf. Phil 3:8), but here we learn that he had a sister and a nephew.</p><p><strong>Marvel at the sovereignty of God here.</strong> The Lord often uses little things and even little children to accomplish his great purposes. This story illustrates the seamless integration between God''s sovereign agenda and human decisions made by responsible people (Johnson, <em>Let''s Study Acts</em>, 288). The nephew hears of the conspiracy. He relates it to Paul. Paul acts wisely. The Roman centurion does his job. And Lysias acts to protect Paul. <strong>There are no burning bushes involved in Paul''s rescue. No light shows.</strong> Paul''s life is spared as a result of people doing what''s in front of them.</p><h3>Point 6: God Uses Lysias and a Roman Army (23:23-35)</h3><p>Lysias immediately responds to the threat. He makes plans to transfer Paul to Caesarea—named after Augustus Caesar, a beautiful harbor on the Mediterranean Sea that served as the headquarters for Roman military forces.</p><ul><li>To protect Paul, Lysias summoned the centurion to prepare two hundred infantrymen, seventy mounted soldiers, and two hundred "spearmen" (v. 23). <strong>This action is a reminder that God can even use secular governments to achieve his purposes.</strong> He turns the hearts of rulers and kings (cf. Prov 21:1).</li><li>Lysias wrote a brief letter to the governor, Felix (vv. 26-30). He calls Felix "most excellent," tries to paint himself as a top-notch soldier, and conveniently omits that he nearly had a Roman citizen flogged illegally. But the most important statement in the letter is <strong>the claim of Paul''s innocence</strong>: the problem involving him revolved around theology; it didn''t warrant death or imprisonment. Luke once again weaves this theme into Acts: Christians aren''t dangerous law-breakers (Schreiner, "Living as Disciples").</li><li>Compare Corinth: after discouragement, the Lord reassures Paul of his presence and protection (18:9-10); angry Jews immediately attack (18:12-13); but Gallio dismisses their complaints as a quibble over theology (cp. 23:29 with 18:12-17).</li><li>The soldiers take Paul to Antipatris by the cover of night, and the seventy horsemen then ride onward with him to Caesarea. <strong>Though they assumed they were moving a prisoner, God was transporting his preacher safely.</strong></li><li>Felix at least begins the right way, promising to hear Paul''s case as soon as the accusers arrive, and sending him to be held by Herod''s praetorian guard (v. 34).</li></ul><p>So the nephew thwarts the plan. Lysias reports the plan. Soldiers transport the prisoner. And all of this occurs under the sovereign rule of the King.</p><h2>Scripture Deep Dive</h2><p>On the Lord''s intimate knowledge of his afflicted servants, Charles Spurgeon commented on this passage:</p><blockquote><p>One is reminded of the Quaker who came to see John Bunyan in prison and said to him, "Friend, the Lord sent me to you and I have been seeking you in half the prisons in England." "No, verily," said John, "that cannot be! For if the Lord had sent you to me, you would have come here at once, for He knows I have been here for years!"</p><p>— Charles Spurgeon, "Paul Cheered in Prison"</p></blockquote><p>Indeed, the Lord knew Paul''s condition, and he knows ours.</p><p>On God''s ordinary means of deliverance:</p><blockquote><p>Sometimes God delivers his children by the simple word of a young relative. Sometimes he has to call in the cavalry. At all times, he is ultimately in charge.</p><p>— Kenneth Gangel, <em>Acts</em>, 391</p></blockquote><p>And on where to look for the comfort Paul received, since we do not receive visions like his — the Lord still speaks to us by his Spirit, through the <em>Christ-centered Scriptures</em>:</p><blockquote><p>This is the whole of what we should seek in the Scriptures: to be well acquainted with Jesus Christ, and the infinite riches which are contained in Him; and which are, by Him, offered to us from God His Father.</p><p>— John Calvin, "Christ the End"</p></blockquote><p>The Pharisees had a category for resurrection but failed to rely on Jesus, who is himself the "resurrection and the life" (John 11:25). <strong>Let your study of Scripture lead you to the hero of Scripture (John 5:39-40,46).</strong></p><h2>Discussion Questions</h2><ol><li>Read Luke 22:63-71 and John 18:19-23. Compare Jesus''s reactions with Paul''s reaction to Ananias. Do you think Paul responded appropriately? Why or why not?</li><li>Read Acts 24:14-16; 26:6-8; and 28:20. Notice how Paul emphasizes the resurrection. How do these passages relate to what Paul told the Sanhedrin in 23:6-10?</li><li>Why must we emphasize the resurrection when sharing the gospel?</li><li>How does verse 11 encourage you? Explain.</li><li>How can we experience the reassuring presence of Jesus on a daily basis?</li><li>Do you find it easier to affirm the sovereignty of God theoretically rather than resting in the sovereignty of God experientially? Explain your answer.</li><li>What does Acts 23:12-30 teach about the way God works to accomplish his purposes?</li><li>How do these assassins compare with modern-day terrorists?</li><li>What does Paul''s life teach about living as law-abiding citizens?</li><li>Take a few moments to pray for God to grant you confidence in his sovereignty and courage in your witness.</li></ol><h2>Application</h2><p><strong>Speak with respect and restraint.</strong> Paul''s example in verses 2-5 cuts both ways: he was right to name injustice, and he was right to step back from the manner in which he named it. Pursue the proper tone with ruling authorities as you address them, refusing to mimic the disrespect so rampant in our culture. And should we fail to speak as we ought, we should emulate Paul''s humility.</p><p><strong>Be an honorable citizen.</strong> Christians shouldn''t be ruthless pragmatists, breaking laws for the sake of our causes and claiming we are acting in the name of God. When the laws of the land don''t hinder us from living out our faith, then we should abide by the rule of law as model citizens.</p><p><strong>This passage shows us that we can rest the whole weight of our concerns on God our Father, who holds the whole world in his hands.</strong> Could it be that you find it easier to affirm the sovereignty of God <em>theoretically</em> rather than resting in the sovereignty of God <em>experientially</em>? God has an infinite number of options for working out his will in our lives. While our daily lives may not look spectacular, we can be assured that God is involved in the affairs of his people. Paul says, "I am sure of this, that he who started a good work in you will carry it on to completion until the day of Jesus Christ" (Phil 1:6). Trust him even in difficult circumstances! Thank him for his care and provision.</p><p><strong>Do what''s in front of you.</strong> God spared Paul''s life through a nephew who spoke up, a centurion who did his job, and a commander who acted. The Lord often uses little things and even little children to accomplish his great purposes. Your ordinary faithfulness this week may be a link in a chain you cannot see.</p><p><strong>Don''t neglect the Scriptures.</strong> We won''t receive a vision like Paul''s in verse 11, but the Lord still speaks by his Spirit through his Word. By marinating our thoughts and hearts in the gospel, we find great strength.</p><hr><h2>Notes</h2><p><strong>Source.</strong> Adapted from <em>Christ-Centered Exposition Commentary</em> on Acts: "Paul in Jerusalem, Part 2" (Acts 22:22–23:11), pages 346–355, and the first half of "Paul in Caesarea, Part 1" (Acts 23:12–24:27), pages 356–360.</p><p><strong>Commentary outlines:</strong></p><p><em>Paul in Jerusalem, Part 2 (Acts 22:22–23:11)</em><br>I. Reactions (22:22–23:10)<br>A. "Paper"—Paul reveals his Roman citizenship (22:22-29) — covered in the Acts 22 session<br>B. "Rock"—Paul rebukes the high priest (22:30–23:5)<br>C. "Scissors"—Paul divides the Pharisees and Sadducees (23:6-10)<br>II. Reassurance (23:11)<br>A. The Lord knows us. B. The Lord is with us. C. The Lord is for us. D. The Lord isn''t finished with us.</p><p><em>Paul in Caesarea, Part 1 (Acts 23:12–24:27)</em><br>I. God Is in Control Even When We''re Going through Trials (23:12-35)<br>A. God uses an unnamed nephew to thwart a plot (23:12-22)<br>B. God uses Lysias to protect Paul (23:23-30)<br>C. God uses a Roman army to transport Paul (23:31-35)<br>II. We Can Be Faithful Witnesses Even in Stressful Situations (24:1-27) — Acts 24 material, not included here</p><p><strong>Memory aid.</strong> The commentary labels Paul''s three reactions "paper," "rock," and "scissors": paper = his Roman citizenship (22:22-29), rock = his rebuke of the high priest (22:30–23:5), scissors = dividing the council over the resurrection (23:6-10).</p><p><strong>Optional supporting passage for the overview.</strong> 2 Corinthians 4:7-9 — "Now we have this treasure in clay jars, so that this extraordinary power may be from God and not from us. We are afflicted in every way but not crushed; we are perplexed but not in despair; we are persecuted but not abandoned; we are struck down but not destroyed" (cf. 2 Cor 6:3-10; 11:23-28; 12:9-12). Also I. Howard Marshall on the underlying conflict: "The real issue is not whether Paul defiled the temple, but whether Judaism was prepared to tolerate Christianity" (<em>Acts of the Apostles</em>, 360).</p><p><strong>Background on Felix (introduced in 23:24-35, developed in Acts 24).</strong> A freed slave; a violent, licentious, and ineffective governor (procurator) of Judea (AD 52–59). He rose to office because of his brother Pallas, for a number of years the head of imperial civil service (Fernando, <em>Acts</em>, 578). He married three women of royal birth; Drusilla—daughter of Herod Agrippa I—was his third wife, a woman of legendary beauty (Thomas, <em>Acts</em>, 671) who apparently divorced her husband in order to marry him. He was eventually recalled to Rome for mishandling riots in Caesarea.</p><p><strong>Textual notes.</strong> On page 357 the commentary prints a doubled "for": "one clearly raised up for ''for such a time as this'' (Esth 4:14)." The quoted phrase from Esther is preserved above; the stray "for" is dropped.</p><p>On page 350 the commentary''s sentence about Ananias''s death reads, as printed, "In less than ten years, Ananias''s Jewish freedom fighters would assassinate him (Polhill, <em>Acts</em>, 468)." This was verified against a high-resolution scan of the page. The point above renders the sense without reproducing the ambiguous possessive.</p><p><strong>Works cited in this session:</strong> Polhill, <em>Acts</em> · Johnson, <em>Let''s Study Acts</em> · Gangel, <em>Acts</em> · Bock, <em>Acts</em> · Marshall, <em>Acts of the Apostles</em> · Schreiner, "Living as Disciples" · Calvin, "Christ the End of the Law" and "Christ the End" · Spurgeon, "Paul Cheered in Prison" · Fernando, <em>Acts</em> · Thomas, <em>Acts</em>.</p>', 1, true, now())
